WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.154 --> 00:00:02.821
(ambient music)

00:00:04.693 --> 00:00:06.873
- There's lots of amazing
weather phenomenon

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.806
happening all over our planet.

00:00:08.806 --> 00:00:13.397
But what's going on
outside of our atmosphere?

00:00:13.397 --> 00:00:16.012
My name is Leland Melvin
and as an astronaut,

00:00:16.012 --> 00:00:18.760
I can tell you weather conditions in space

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.388
can be a matter of life and death.

00:00:21.388 --> 00:00:23.971
Especially during a space walk.

00:00:27.926 --> 00:00:29.887
When we prepare for a space walk,

00:00:29.887 --> 00:00:32.643
we have to be able to
deal with temperatures

00:00:32.643 --> 00:00:37.160
that range from positive 250
degrees Fahrenheit in the sun

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.077
and minus 250 degrees in the shade.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.669
But in our space suit we have the ability

00:00:44.669 --> 00:00:47.468
to withstand those temperature extremes

00:00:47.468 --> 00:00:49.715
because we have visors
that reflect the sun

00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:52.567
away from our eyes and
we have heated gloves

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:54.248
as well as a heated suit,

00:00:54.248 --> 00:00:57.894
and then we have cooling
that allows us to stay cool

00:00:57.894 --> 00:01:00.727
during those temperature extremes.

00:01:01.958 --> 00:01:04.434
A solar flare is a sudden
flash of brightness,

00:01:04.434 --> 00:01:06.227
observed near the sun’s surface

00:01:06.227 --> 00:01:09.390
that emits X-ray and UV radiation.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.520
In space, the radiation
levels can be deadly.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.370
When we're notified of a solar
flare on the space station

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.969
we retreat to what we call the doghouse.

00:01:18.969 --> 00:01:22.076
This doghouse is lined with water bags

00:01:22.076 --> 00:01:24.779
that allow us to block
the radiation coming

00:01:24.779 --> 00:01:27.782
from the sun before it gets to our bodies.

00:01:27.782 --> 00:01:28.730
And that's where we go to keep

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:32.563
from getting exposed to
high-energy radiation.

00:01:35.087 --> 00:01:37.894
As we go further out
into our solar system,

00:01:37.894 --> 00:01:41.561
weather events become
more and more extreme.

00:01:43.326 --> 00:01:46.555
Jupiter, our solar
system's biggest planet,

00:01:46.555 --> 00:01:49.754
also features its most impressive storm.

00:01:49.754 --> 00:01:51.337
The great red spot.

00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:56.037
The gigantic tempest is
twice as wide as planet Earth

00:01:56.037 --> 00:02:00.155
and has winds that reach
up to 400 miles per hour.

00:02:00.155 --> 00:02:04.322
It's been swirling over
Jupiter for at least 150 years.

00:02:06.838 --> 00:02:10.660
Further out, Neptune features
its own great big storm.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:15.056
It takes up the planet's
entire atmosphere.

00:02:15.056 --> 00:02:18.422
Neptune is our solar
system's windiest planet.

00:02:18.422 --> 00:02:22.800
With wind speeds that can reach
up to 1,500 miles per hour.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.993
That's nine times faster
than the strongest winds

00:02:25.993 --> 00:02:27.910
ever recorded on Earth.

00:02:29.476 --> 00:02:33.029
To find the strongest known
winds in our universe,

00:02:33.029 --> 00:02:36.561
we have to go well
beyond our solar system.

00:02:36.561 --> 00:02:38.710
Astrophysicists at York University

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.486
have discovered the
fastest winds ever recorded

00:02:41.486 --> 00:02:43.843
near a super massive black hole.

00:02:43.843 --> 00:02:44.834
These winds speeds clock in
at 125 million miles per hour.

00:02:44.834 --> 00:02:49.001
These winds speeds clock in
at 125 million miles per hour.

00:02:49.981 --> 00:02:54.148
I hope my crew never gets
caught up in anything like that.

